  "data_url": "https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.32916761",
  "verified": {
    "opened_data_files": true,
    "what_i_actually_checked": "Opened the supplementary workbook (41588_2026_2735_MOESM4_ESM.xlsx, 56 sheets) and read its table of contents plus Supplementary Tables 1, 14, 15 and 17: 24 input disease GWAS (genome-wide association studies) with download links, and 172/537/170/140 independent risk loci for the cardiovascular-, metabolic-, gastrointestinal- and immune-MDD (major depressive disorder) factors. Fetched the figshare readme.txt (615 bytes): columns are CHR, BP, A1, A2, beta, SE, MAF, P, SNP, Z, genome build GRCh37. Pulled the first 400 KB of two of the eight .tsv.gz summary-statistic files by byte-range request and decompressed the first gzip block: the header line is identical (CHR/BP/A1/A2/beta/SE/MAF/P/SNP/Z) in both the Q-SNP-removed and the non-removed versions, so no heterogeneity (Q) statistic is shipped as a column. Confirmed the figshare record is CC BY 4.0 and downloads with no login. Checked the Zenodo record 21711189: it holds only scripts.zip (33 KB), no data.",
    "what_came_from_paper_text_only": "Per-factor SNP (single nucleotide polymorphism) totals (6.47M-6.91M), effective sample sizes (826k-926k), the 47% of MDD SNP-heritability figure and the gut-brain conclusion all come from the text and Supplementary Table 14; I did not download whole files to count rows because each is 227-318 MB, over the 50 MB cap. That the multiple sclerosis GWAS is available only on request, and that the MDD GWAS comes from a separate iPSYCH download page, also comes from the data-availability statement only."
  },
  "part_a_killers": {
    "a1_downloadable": {"pass": true, "note": "Verified: figshare 32916761 is CC BY 4.0, open, no login; a byte-range request returned valid gzip data. Eight files, 227-318 MB each."},
    "a2_label_is_a_column": {"pass": true, "note": "For the obvious framing the label (beta, SE, Z, P per SNP for each depression-disease latent factor) is a column, verified in the file header. For the more interesting 'shared versus disease-specific variant' framing it is not: that label only exists as the set difference between the Q-SNP-removed and non-removed files."},
    "a3_inputs_present": {"pass": true, "note": "Weak pass: the files carry no predictive features at all, only chromosome, position, alleles and rsID. Features would have to come from a reference genome and annotation databases, but GRCh37 coordinates resolve deterministically from one FASTA download, not thousands of lookups."},
    "a4_units_stated": {"pass": true, "note": "Verified in readme.txt: beta, standard error, minor allele frequency, P-value, Z, genome build GRCh37. Standard GWAS units, consistent across all eight files."},
    "a5_enough_rows": {"pass": true, "n_rows": 6470160, "note": "Rows pass on paper (6.47M-6.91M SNPs per factor, from Supplementary Table 14) but the independent unit is the LD (linkage disequilibrium) block: 1,019 genome-wide-significant independent loci across all four factors, and ~1,700 LD blocks genome-wide. Pass numerically, with that caveat."},
    "a6_fits_laptop": {"pass": true, "working_set": "~1.2 GB gzipped for the four Q-SNP-removed factor files, roughly 3-4 GB uncompressed; subsettable by chromosome or to HapMap3 SNPs", "note": "Fits a 16 GB laptop if read per chromosome; no GPU needed for a gradient-boosting baseline on annotations."},
    "a7_counts_match_paper": {"pass": true, "note": "Could not verify by download (files exceed the 50 MB cap). Supplementary Table 14's internal numbers are self-consistent (e.g. immune factor 6,501,113 SNPs without Q-SNP removal minus 6,470,160 with = 30,953 removed) and nothing contradicts them."},
    "a8_label_provenance": {"pass": false, "note": "FAILS. The per-SNP beta/Z for each depression-disease factor is not a measurement: it is the output of genomic structural equation modelling (a statistical model) fitted to 24 public GWAS whose download links are given in Supplementary Table 1. The common-factor Z is close to a weighted linear combination of those input Z-scores, so the natural feature set reconstructs the label by construction. Every other candidate label in the paper is likewise a model output (TWAS/FUSION gene hits, MAGMA and GSA-MiXeR enrichments, gsMAP cell-type scores), and the Q-SNP class label is a power-dependent significance threshold, not a measured quantity."
  },
